WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.270
LUCAS MOREL: Hi.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.060
I'm Lucas Morel, the John
K. Boardman junior professor

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.950
of politics and
head of the Politics

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:21.020
Department at Washington
and Lee University

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.700
in Lexington, Virginia.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.410
I'm also a visiting
teaching fellow

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.980
at the Kirby Center, the School
of Government in Washington,

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.020
DC, for Hillsdale College.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.470
Welcome to this symposium
on race in America.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:38.360
I'll be speaking today on
a colorblind constitution.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.270
Abraham Lincoln once said
that the central idea

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.240
of American public opinion,
from the beginning,

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.760
was the equality of men.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.680
The problems, of
course, arose when

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.040
the idea was undermined
by the continued practice

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.940
of racial slavery
as it was defended,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.780
no longer as a necessary
evil, but as a positive good,

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.860
if you can believe it-- good
for the master and the slave.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.450
Even though the
defenders of slavery

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.600
were defeated in the
Civil War, white supremacy

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.820
still reigned
throughout the South

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.770
well into the 20th century.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.100
And so a nation that
began as the first

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.370
in human history devoted
to the self-evident truth

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:23.480
that all men are created equal
still wrestles with the role

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.740
that race should or should
not play in government policy.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.200
Put simply, should
Americans govern themselves

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.610
in a colorblind fashion when
it comes to the rule of law,

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:38.960
or should we use race to try
to eliminate the lingering

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.680
effects of racial
slavery and segregation?

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.770
Put simply, should
Americans govern themselves

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.700
in a colorblind fashion when
it comes to the rule of law,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.280
or should we use race
to try to eliminate

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.690
the lingering effects--

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.310
the legacy, as they say--

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.740
of racial slavery
and segregation?

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.030
One of the greatest American
orators of the 19th century,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:07.170
this gentleman, an escaped
slave and abolitionist orator

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.680
Frederick Douglass,
began his public career

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.710
criticizing the United
States Constitution

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.170
as a pro-slavery document.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.990
Douglass asked,
what country have I?

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:21.870
The institutions of this
country do not know me.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.020
They do not recognize
me as a man.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.130
And so he concluded, I
have no love for America.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:32.340
To his credit, and this
country's, he did not

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.570
hold that position for long.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.370
He eventually claimed
America as his country,

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.310
calling the principles of the
Declaration of Independence

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.030
saving principles, and
the Constitution no longer

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:49.260
as pro-slavery but a
glorious liberty document.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.300
He found that reading
the Constitution the way

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.820
his former mentor,
William Lloyd Garrison,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.700
read it, as a
pro-slavery document,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.460
contradicted its
preamble and gave

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.690
short shrift to the many levers
of freedom found therein.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.540
He also noticed that the
Constitution actually never

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.070
mentioned the word "slave"
or "slavery" or "race,"

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:14.370
and it permitted the
Congress to actually ban

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.370
the importation of
slaves as early as 1808,

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.710
which they actually did.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:23.790
He said, therefore, that it
leans to freedom, not slavery,

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.730
and looked to the
abolition of slavery

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.670
rather than its perpetuity.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:35.070
Planting his political identity
firmly in his citizenship,

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.070
it led this former slave,
who had every reason

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:43.470
to hate America, to become
one of her greatest defenders.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.900
On the question of race
in the Constitution,

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.670
Frederick Douglass was clear.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:51.210
Quote, "Color should not be
the criterion of rights,"

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:52.540
end quote.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.210
He also said, "I
know of no rights

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:58.110
of race superior to the
rights of humanity."

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.470
When asked what he would do if
he were president of the United

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.170
States, after he stopped
laughing because that was

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:07.560
absurd at that time-- he
knew it would never happen--

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.010
he replied, I would secure to
every citizen of the United

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.160
States, without
regard to color or sex

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.500
or religion or race, equal
protection of the laws.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.230
If all men are created
equal, then their government

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.340
should protect them
equally, secure

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.770
what each member of
the political community

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.150
possesses equally, namely,
their natural rights.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.850
Douglass's plain reading
of the Constitution

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.920
and the Declaration
of Independence

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.830
led him to conclude that
Blacks should not be

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.400
made an exception in the law.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.350
This would reinforce
the stereotype

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.950
of racial inferiority and
undermine the central idea

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.500
that all men are created equal.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.230
Douglass always
considered the impact

00:04:55.230 --> 00:05:00.690
that public policy would have
on how whites treated and viewed

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.340
Black Americans.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.520
And therefore, he didn't
favor any government practice

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.070
that would relieve Black
Americans of any duty

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.920
or responsibility
that was expected

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.020
of all other Americans.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.830
"We do not solicit
unusual favor," he said,

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:20.940
"but will be content with
rough handed fair play."

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.420
He went on to add, "We are
neither lame or blind that we

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.210
should seek to throw
off the responsibility

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.330
of our own existence
or to cast ourselves

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.650
upon public charity
for support."

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:37.390
But as much as Douglass did to
promote the elevation of Blacks

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.210
in America, he
actually was not a fan

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.420
of what we would
today call race pride.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:49.110
He called it a false
foundation and a positive evil.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:52.320
He proclaimed it the very
thing we're fighting against.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.750
For most of American
history, Black Americans

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:58.980
wanted nothing to do with the
color line that separated them

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.010
from the rest of the
American population.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.790
He wrote that it was our
enemies who sought to, quote,

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:09.330
"deepen and widen the
line of separation

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:14.010
between the white and colored
people of this country."

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:16.770
Given that Blacks were
a numerical minority

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.250
in the United States,
racial solidarity

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:25.050
was never the key to gaining
greater security and protection

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.290
of their civil and
political rights.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:30.390
His argument against
race pride led

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:34.920
him to criticize what he called
complexional institutions.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.920
Over a decade before Lincoln
was elected president,

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.370
Douglass argued, quote, that
"all distinctions founded

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:46.170
on complexion ought to
be repealed, repudiated,

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.450
and forever abolished.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.460
Every right,
privilege, and immunity

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.390
now enjoyed by the white man
ought to be as freely granted

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:57.890
to the man of color."

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:00.650
He thought that, for example,
colored conventions, as they

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:02.870
called it then,
colored exhibitions,

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.330
colored associations
and institutions--

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:08.840
he said these are hindrances
rather than helps in achieving

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:12.680
a higher and better
estimation for the Black man

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.810
in the public mind.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:19.730
His concern for the impact of
public policy on public opinion

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:24.410
led him to argue against any
recognition of race or color

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.940
when it came to
government policy.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.720
It's safe to say
that Douglass was not

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:33.320
a practitioner of
identity politics.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:39.440
So have the American people ever
adopted Douglass's clear belief

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.160
in a colorblind Constitution?

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.100
After the 14th Amendment was
ratified just a few years

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:47.710
after the end of the Civil
War, the Supreme Court

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:53.200
began to decide cases involving
equal treatment under the law.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.570
One of the key clauses
of the 14th Amendment

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:58.630
requires that no state
shall, quote, "deny

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:01.240
to any person within
its jurisdiction

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.750
the equal protection of
the laws," end quote.

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.540
This would seem to
ban any government

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.420
use of racial classifications.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.790
But unlike Douglass,
who did not believe

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:17.390
race should be the measure of
anyone's constitutional rights,

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:20.690
to this day, the
Supreme Court has never

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:25.850
adopted or interpreted
the Constitution to be

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:28.220
a colorblind Constitution.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.550
Instead, it has reserved
to itself the authority

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:36.590
to decide when government
use of race is constitutional

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.480
and when it is not.

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:41.419
Ironically, it was a
lone dissenting opinion

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:46.350
that introduced the concept
of a colorblind Constitution.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.200
However, that dissenting
justice was outnumbered

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.440
by 7 justices who interpreted
the Constitution to permit

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:56.400
government use of race as long
as it was done in, as they

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.180
call it, a reasonable fashion.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.750
What was later known as the
rational basis test permitted

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:07.170
large discretion on the
part of state governments

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.950
to employ racial classifications
to promote the peace, safety,

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:14.920
and comfort of the community.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.750
That case was
Plessy v. Ferguson,

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:23.050
an 1896 ruling that gave us the
notion that separate but equal

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:24.370
was all right.

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:27.340
It was an acceptable measure
of whether one's race was

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:32.110
being used in a constitutional
way by state government.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:36.130
This pernicious standard was
precedent for subsequent cases

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:39.760
until the landmark decision
in Brown v. Board of Education

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.850
of Topeka, Kansas in 1954.

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:47.290
It ruled unanimously that racial
segregation of public schools

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:50.950
deprives students of,
quote, "the equal protection

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:56.490
of the laws guaranteed by the
14th Amendment," end quote.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.280
The NAACP, the National
Association for the Advancement

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:03.420
of Colored People, they provided
the lead group of attorneys

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.130
in these cases.

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:09.450
Their in-house legal defense
fund, known as The Fund,

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:14.670
had for decades not attacked
segregation laws directly,

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.550
but had argued, and
argued successfully,

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.500
that segregation
was unconstitutional

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:24.570
because it did not apply equally
to citizens of all races.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:26.970
They adopted this
strategy early on because

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:29.490
of the precedent of
separate but equal,

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:33.330
the separate but equal doctrine
established in the 1896 case

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.370
of Plessy v. Ferguson.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:38.790
So the NAACP contested
legal segregation

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:43.000
not by arguing that it
itself was unconstitutional,

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.630
but only if they separated
citizens according

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:49.990
to race in an unequal way.

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:53.360
The idea was to make
segregation too expensive,

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:57.820
especially for Southern state
governments to maintain.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:01.750
If a state provided a public
park, a library, a pool, a golf

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:04.300
course, but only did
so-- it was public,

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:09.160
but only did so for whites, but
it didn't provide one equally

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:11.230
for Black citizens,
or they provided

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:15.010
one of inferior quality,
the NAACP would say, ah,

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:18.280
this violates the 14th
Amendment which says, again,

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:22.060
nor shall any state deny to any
person within its jurisdiction

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:25.750
the equal protection
of the laws.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:30.100
In case after case, in the
first half of the 20th century,

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:34.120
the NAACP won court
case after court case

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:37.570
in the highest Court in the
land using this strategy,

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:40.540
showing that racial
segregation was never applied--

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:42.580
or not applied equally.

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:45.970
And it forced states to
provide equal accommodations

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.880
or, if they lacked the
funds, to eliminate

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:52.470
the segregated
facility altogether.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:55.590
Well, the problem was that after
a while, Southern states that

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.120
had been segregating
but could not

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.430
afford to do so
on an equal basis,

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.770
they just decided to
close it all down,

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:05.490
close the public facility
rather than desegregate them

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:09.060
or provide an equivalent
one for Black citizens.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:13.050
More importantly, in
terms of the jurisprudence

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:18.090
that the NAACP was pursuing
here, with each victory,

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:21.870
they were actually
reinforcing legal segregation

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:25.020
by reinforcing the precedent
of separate but equal

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:28.720
that was established
way back in 1896.

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:32.160
So by the 1940s, the
Legal Defense Fund finally

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:36.000
decided to attack
segregation head on,

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.600
and they invited the Supreme
Court to overturn its decision

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:40.950
in Plessy.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.350
Now, this is a risky--
or was a risky strategy,

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:45.630
given how much the
high Court likes

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:47.480
to stick to previous decisions.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:49.710
They don't like to admit
that they were ever wrong,

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:54.360
especially precedents that had
been on the books for so long.

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.240
In all four lower
court cases dealing

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:00.030
with segregated
schools, the courts

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:04.020
had followed the Plessy
doctrine of separate but equal.

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:07.710
They ruled that if the
conditions of the schools

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:10.800
were basically equal,
the Constitution was not

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:14.460
violated even if the
educational outcomes,

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:19.220
the results of that segregated
education, were unequal.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:24.650
Why were the lower courts
so focused on the inputs--

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:26.690
for example,
buildings, curricula,

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:30.110
qualifications of the
teachers, their salaries--

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:33.500
rather than the
effect that segregated

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:37.610
or separate but equal conditions
were having on children?

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.070
For this, we have to go back
to the court's reasoning

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:41.300
in Plessy.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.730
So some quick facts
about the Plessy case.

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:49.310
Homer Plessy was 7/8 Caucasian
and 1/8 African blood,

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:53.790
as the testimony said, though
the African-ness, if you will,

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.310
was not discernible.

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:57.710
You couldn't tell by
looking at Homer Plessy

00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.870
that he was part Black.

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:02.840
He deliberately rode
in a railroad car

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:04.700
in Louisiana that
was only traveling

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:06.380
within the state of Louisiana.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:10.760
He deliberately rode in a
car for white passengers

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:13.550
and actually had to tip
the conductor to the fact

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:16.640
that, oh, by the
way, I'm 1/8 Black.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:18.140
So this was a contrived case.

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:19.700
He did this deliberately
so that this

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:21.500
could be thrown into court.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:23.780
So Homer Plessy, a local
citizen's committee,

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:25.400
actually worked
with the railroads

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:27.710
because they thought it
was expensive to segregate.

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:29.280
They didn't want this either.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:31.740
So they worked to try to
get this case to the Supreme

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.820
Court, which they did.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.740
You have to remember,
at this time,

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:39.285
even though he was 1/8
Black, we were operating--

00:14:39.285 --> 00:14:40.910
this country was
operating under what's

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:42.770
known as the one drop rule.

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.510
So it didn't matter
how Caucasian you were.

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:49.190
If you had one drop of
a particular other race

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:52.142
or ethnicity, that's
what they counted you as.

00:14:52.142 --> 00:14:53.850
And you can trace this
back-- good grief,

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:59.730
Florida had a one drop rule all
the way back dating to 1866.

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:01.680
An important part
of the Plessy case

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:05.160
was the text of the
1890 Louisiana statute

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.777
that required separate
railway carriages for whites

00:15:07.777 --> 00:15:09.360
and what they called
the colored race.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:12.330
And this is what the text
says, that all railway

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:15.990
companies carrying passengers
in their coaches in this state

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:21.000
shall provide equal but
separate accommodations

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:24.150
for the white and colored
races by providing

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:27.960
two or more passenger coaches
for each passenger train

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:30.480
or by dividing the passenger
coaches by a partition

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:34.470
so as to secure
equal accommodations.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.900
And it added-- and this
is very important--

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:42.150
no person or persons shall
be admitted to occupy seats

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:45.720
in coaches other than the ones
assigned to them on account

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:48.790
of the race they belong to.

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:51.940
Justice Brown interpreted
the Equal Protection

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:54.190
clause of the 14th
Amendment, quote,

00:15:54.190 --> 00:15:58.750
"to enforce the absolute
equality of the two races

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:01.090
before the law," end quote.

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:03.940
However, he read this
to mandate equality

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:07.840
of treatment, not
equality of outcome

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:10.600
or social or public perception.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:14.590
Just because whites must
sit in this railway carriage

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:17.980
and Blacks must sit in
that railway carriage,

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:22.180
he says this does not impose
a badge of inferiority

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:23.860
on either race.

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:26.560
Moreover, state
legislatures, he said,

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.270
had long been
allowed to separate

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:31.750
the races in public venues
as part of their traditional

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:34.180
what is known as police powers.

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:36.280
Justice Brown gives
the example, actually,

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:39.490
of segregated schools,
even in the states where

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:41.740
the rights of Blacks
had been, quote,

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:44.650
"most earnestly
enforced," end quote.

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:47.660
So this was a
longstanding practice.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:49.760
Justice Brown gets to
the crux of the issue

00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:53.180
when he says the case comes
down to whether the statute

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:57.570
of Louisiana is a
reasonable regulation.

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.190
His answer?

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:03.630
There must necessarily
be a large discretion

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:05.849
on the part of the
legislature, which

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:10.079
he argues is at liberty
to act with reference

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:15.790
to established usages, customs,
and traditions of the people,

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:17.970
and with a view to the
promotion of their comfort

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:22.020
and the preservation of the
public peace and good order.

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:24.119
Now, all of this, of
course, was a euphemism

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:26.220
for maintaining the
status quo, which,

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.620
of course, was white supremacy.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:32.520
Given this practice, the
Court concluded that, quote,

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:36.840
"we cannot say that a law which
authorizes or even requires

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:40.110
the separation of the two
races in public conveyances is

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:43.830
unreasonable or more obnoxious
to the 14th Amendment than

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:47.280
the acts of Congress requiring
separate schools for colored

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:49.380
children in the
District of Columbia,

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.810
the constitutionality of which
does not seem to have been

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:55.800
questioned, or the corresponding
acts of state legislatures,"

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.300
end quote.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:59.550
So this reasonableness
standard, what we now

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:02.100
call the rational
basis test, it actually

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:05.040
is the lowest threshold
for state action

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:09.180
to be considered constitutional
or unconstitutional, especially

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:12.510
under the 14th Amendment's
Equal Protection Clause.

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:15.960
So the court therefore allows
large discretion by the states

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:21.120
if they employ, at that time and
for decades, racial segregation

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:25.200
when regulating public
interaction between the races.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:28.980
But what if racial segregation
produces a perception

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.290
that one race is
inferior to another,

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:33.330
a badge of inferiority?

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:36.420
Justice Brown addresses
this directly in a passage

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.570
that Chief Justice
Earl Warren will later

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:42.790
reject to arrive at the
opposite conclusion.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.730
And this is 60 years
later in the Brown case.

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:48.090
So here's what the
Plessy Court concluded.

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:50.280
We consider the
underlying fallacy

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:53.730
of the plaintiff's argument
to consist in the assumption

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:56.910
that the enforced
separation of the two races

00:18:56.910 --> 00:19:01.530
stamps the colored race
with a badge of inferiority.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:04.350
If this be so, it
is not by reason

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:08.490
of anything found in the act
or the statute, but solely

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:12.690
because the colored race
chooses to put that construction

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:15.270
or that interpretation upon it.

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:18.240
In other words, any
feeling of inferiority

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:20.250
as a result of
racial segregation

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:23.580
is not the product
of government policy

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:28.320
as long as that racial
policy is equally applied.

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:31.980
The Court's Separate but Equal
doctrine does not actually

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:35.550
make any citizen inferior.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.410
Therefore, if someone
does feel inferior

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:41.100
because of a racial
classification,

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:43.170
that's because
that person chooses

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:46.080
to interpret it that way.

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:50.610
In the one dissenting opinion,
the opinion of Justice John

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:54.840
Marshall Harlan, he thinks that
this reading of the statute

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:56.190
is absurd.

00:19:56.190 --> 00:20:00.330
Everyone knows that this law
and other segregation laws

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:03.930
are intended to maintain
a legal, or at least

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:07.650
a social hierarchy of
whites over Blacks.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:11.700
He also doesn't think courts,
especially the Supreme Court,

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:14.640
should inquire into the
so-called reasonableness

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.730
or wisdom of state legislatures.

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:20.190
The only question the Supreme
Court should try to answer,

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:23.520
he thought, was, does
the state law comport

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:24.570
with the Constitution?

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:26.653
And in this case, it's the
Equal Protection Clause

00:20:26.653 --> 00:20:28.290
of the 14th Amendment.

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:33.630
In his mind, the Court
recognizes no caste or superior

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:37.500
ruling class of citizens.

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:40.650
He then cited a concept that
was actually first presented

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:44.700
at oral argument by the lead
attorney for Homer Plessy,

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:47.760
and that was a guy
named Albion Tourgee.

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:49.800
At oral argument,
he observed, quote,

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:55.500
"If justice is pictured
blind, then the law, at least,

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:58.520
should be colorblind."

00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:02.830
Here's how Justice Harlan
put it in his dissent.

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:09.210
"Our Constitution is colorblind
and neither knows nor tolerates

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:11.400
classes among citizens.

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:15.000
In respect of civil
rights, all citizens

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:17.710
are equal before the law."

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:19.930
He added, "The law
regards man as man

00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:22.870
and takes no account
of his surroundings

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:27.340
or of his color when his
civil rights are guaranteed

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:31.250
by the supreme law of the land."

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:36.440
So given so many subsequent
cases that relied upon Plessy

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:40.460
as precedent, how did
the Warren court in 1954

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:45.370
rule unanimously to reject the
separate but equal doctrine?

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:47.860
In its short-- I think
it's nine pages--

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:52.420
plainly written ruling, the
new Chief Justice, Justice Earl

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:55.910
Warren, argued that Americans
in the mid 20th century

00:21:55.910 --> 00:22:00.880
now lived in a different world
than that of the 1896 Plessy

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:02.280
Court.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:08.510
Most importantly, education
was now a leading function

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:10.100
of state government.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:14.720
It was now compulsory, supported
by a substantial budget,

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:18.680
and had become, as Warren
put it, the very foundation

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:21.140
of good citizenship.

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:24.860
So if the state became the
educator of its citizens,

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:26.570
then it was
responsible, according

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:30.530
to Warren, not just for
the educational inputs

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:33.230
but for what those
inputs produced,

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:34.565
the educational outcomes.

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:39.560
Such an opportunity,
Earl Warren said,

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:43.040
where the state has
undertaken to provide it,

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:47.360
is a right which must
be made available to all

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:49.430
on equal terms.

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:52.400
To get around the Plessy
doctrine of separate but equal,

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:55.840
Chief Justice Warren leaned
on more recent precedents--

00:22:55.840 --> 00:23:01.290
Sweatt v. Painter and
McLaurin v. Oklahoma, 1950--

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:06.030
to include what he called
intangible considerations

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:09.000
in the educational
process, not just inputs,

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:11.910
material ones like buildings
or teacher qualifications,

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:15.280
things you could measure, but
also the learning environment

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:16.570
and-- this is key--

00:23:16.570 --> 00:23:20.730
what that environment
produced, the results.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.230
As he put it, we
must look instead

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:25.860
to the effect of segregation--

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:30.630
segregation itself, its
effect on public education.

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:35.230
He concluded that
to separate children

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:39.520
from others of similar age and
qualifications solely because

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:45.250
of their race generates
a feeling of inferiority

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:47.590
as to their status
in the community that

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:52.030
may affect their hearts and
minds in a way unlikely ever

00:23:52.030 --> 00:23:54.490
to be undone.

00:23:54.490 --> 00:23:58.230
So when governments segregate
children according to race

00:23:58.230 --> 00:24:02.490
and this separation produces
feelings of inferiority,

00:24:02.490 --> 00:24:06.270
then government is now
responsible for that outcome.

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:09.510
It's not simply an
interpretation or construction

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:12.690
that children choose to
put on their racially

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:14.910
segregated school.

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:17.910
The Court ruled that in the
field of public education,

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:22.390
the doctrine of separate
but equal has no place.

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:28.750
Separate educational facilities
are inherently unequal.

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:32.430
Now, few Court observers
disputed the decision--

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:34.680
the result in this case.

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:37.680
But many questioned the
reasoning, especially

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.710
its reliance upon
psychological tests

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:43.590
to determine the effects
of racial segregation

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:45.690
upon segregated students.

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:48.930
To decide if the Constitution
was violated by a government

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:53.690
use of race on the basis
of feelings generated

00:24:53.690 --> 00:24:57.060
seemed like a dubious
basis for determining

00:24:57.060 --> 00:25:03.930
if a person's rights under the
Constitution were infringed.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:05.040
Think about it.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:09.090
What if a segregated
school didn't generate

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:11.980
a feeling of inferiority?

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:14.590
Would there be no violation
of the Equal Protection

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:16.930
Clause of the 14th Amendment?

00:25:16.930 --> 00:25:22.670
And if not, did Brown
actually overturn Plessy?

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:26.550
Now, if Brown decided
that government could not

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:29.540
pay attention to race,
couldn't separate

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:31.280
students on the
basis of race in K

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:34.790
through 12 schools, public
schools, the second case

00:25:34.790 --> 00:25:39.260
involving education ruled
that government could use race

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:45.190
in higher education, not
to separate but to promote

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:49.420
diversity, what they call the
educational benefits that flow

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:52.120
from a racially
diverse student body.

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:54.340
So we now shift our
discussion of government use

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:59.170
of racial classifications from
segregation or desegregation,

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:03.010
to the policy known today
as affirmative action.

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:04.930
It should go without
saying that using race

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:08.620
in a constitutional way means
you're not operating according

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:11.230
to a colorblind Constitution.

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:13.630
We'll see how the
Supreme Court justifies

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:18.100
this under the 14th Amendment's
Equal Protection Clause.

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:21.550
The key case is actually
two cases in 2003

00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:24.280
involving the University
of Michigan, their law

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:27.830
school and their
undergraduate university.

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:30.640
They are the cases of Grutter
v. Bollinger and Gratz

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:33.540
v. Bollinger, respectively.

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:36.200
Now, the Supreme Court
upheld affirmative action

00:26:36.200 --> 00:26:38.480
as it was practiced
in the law school,

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:40.790
but it rejected
affirmative action

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:45.890
that operated differently at
the undergraduate university.

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:50.360
The key precedent for
Justice Sandra Day O'Connor,

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:53.750
who took all the Supreme Court
affirmative action cases when

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:55.400
she was on the court--

00:26:55.400 --> 00:26:58.640
her key precedent was a case
that had been on the books

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:03.140
for 25 years, the 1978
case of Regents v. Bakke.

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:05.780
Unlike the Brown
decision, which only

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:09.410
relied on the 14th
Amendment back in 1954,

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:13.820
O'Connor's opinion drew from two
provisions of the Constitution,

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:16.610
and in this she was
following Justice Powell

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:19.880
in Bakke-- the Equal Protection
Clause of the 14th Amendment

00:27:19.880 --> 00:27:23.120
for sure, but also,
because it dealt

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:26.240
with higher education,
the Free Speech

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:29.450
Clause of the First Amendment.

00:27:29.450 --> 00:27:31.850
Reasonableness or the
Rational Basis Test

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:33.950
was the prevailing
Court standard

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:37.400
until about World War II,
when two cases dealing

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:41.540
with Japanese internment,
the Hirabayashi case in 1943

00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:44.210
and the Korematsu case of 1944--

00:27:44.210 --> 00:27:49.550
these led the Court to adopt
a new, more rigorous measure

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:52.715
of constitutionality
when it came to race.

00:27:52.715 --> 00:27:54.650
Racial classifications
by government

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:59.510
were now considered by the
Court immediately suspect.

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:02.690
They weren't categorically
unconstitutional.

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:04.310
They've never ruled that.

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:07.700
But they were immediately
suspect and subject to,

00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:12.330
as the Court put it,
the most rigid scrutiny.

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:14.060
So they're moving
off of reasonableness

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:16.640
and ratcheting it up a bit.

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:20.060
This is now known as the
strict scrutiny test,

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:23.150
and it's a test that has
two prongs, both of which

00:28:23.150 --> 00:28:27.110
have to pass in order for
a government use of race

00:28:27.110 --> 00:28:29.480
to be considered constitutional.

00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:34.920
Number one, it has to serve
a compelling state interest.

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:37.820
It has to be a priority
of the state government.

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:40.550
And that also includes the
federal government now.

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:44.510
Secondly, it has to
be narrowly tailored

00:28:44.510 --> 00:28:47.790
to meet that state interest.

00:28:47.790 --> 00:28:50.570
So you have a constitutionally
permissible end

00:28:50.570 --> 00:28:54.200
that you're looking for, and
a corresponding constitutional

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:58.705
means to securing that
end if you're using race.

00:28:58.705 --> 00:29:00.080
So at the University
of Michigan,

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:01.730
affirmative action
was used, again,

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:03.920
one way at the law
school and another way

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:05.870
at the undergraduate university.

00:29:05.870 --> 00:29:08.180
The difference between
the two policies

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:11.360
produced the different
outcomes, as O'Connor, and then

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:16.710
Rehnquist in the Gratz case,
applied strict scrutiny.

00:29:16.710 --> 00:29:19.280
So what were they doing
at the law school?

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:23.990
They were looking at students
according to race, especially

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:29.290
those that they considered
underrepresented minorities.

00:29:29.290 --> 00:29:32.240
We'll use the acronym URM.

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:35.210
So they would favor
URMs to produce

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:39.260
what they called a critical mass
or meaningful representation

00:29:39.260 --> 00:29:41.962
of Blacks, Hispanics,
and Native Americans.

00:29:41.962 --> 00:29:43.670
And if you listen to
the oral arguments--

00:29:43.670 --> 00:29:47.540
I remember being on the
Interstate 66 on my way to DC

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:48.620
and actually listening.

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:50.245
I think this was one
of the first court

00:29:50.245 --> 00:29:52.215
cases where they actually--

00:29:52.215 --> 00:29:53.840
I think this was on
NPR-- they actually

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:57.180
let you listen to what
was actually being said.

00:29:57.180 --> 00:29:59.060
The Michigan attorney
was fantastic.

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:01.143
Whatever they paid her,
they should have given her

00:30:01.143 --> 00:30:04.072
a raise, because Scalia and
the conservative justices

00:30:04.072 --> 00:30:05.030
were leaning in on her.

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:08.480
They were trying to see if
they could say that Michigan

00:30:08.480 --> 00:30:10.280
was employing a quota.

00:30:10.280 --> 00:30:12.290
And they said,
hmm, critical mass.

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:14.450
Could you define
critical mass for us?

00:30:14.450 --> 00:30:15.470
And she said, yeah--

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:17.640
meaningful
representation of URMs.

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:19.070
And they were like, heh heh heh!

00:30:19.070 --> 00:30:20.445
What's meaningful
representation?

00:30:20.445 --> 00:30:24.680
So if she used an absolute
number or a fixed percentage

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:27.280
or a narrow band that
they were looking for,

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:28.220
they would have lost.

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:30.590
Even the liberals on the
court would have said,

00:30:30.590 --> 00:30:31.730
you can't do that.

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:32.670
You know what she did?

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:34.330
She just said, critical mass.

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:36.080
She just bounced back
and forth-- critical

00:30:36.080 --> 00:30:37.455
mass, meaningful
representation--

00:30:37.455 --> 00:30:39.050
until the justices gave up.

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:41.030
So critical mass.

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:42.920
Second, the state
of Michigan argued

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:44.540
that it had a
compelling interest

00:30:44.540 --> 00:30:47.540
to diversify the
student body in order

00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:50.760
to enhance the education
of its law students.

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:52.820
So how do you get
educational diversity,

00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:54.500
free exchange of ideas?

00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:58.660
You can use race to
help promote that.

00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:01.380
Lastly, it claimed that its
pursuit of a critical mass

00:31:01.380 --> 00:31:05.790
was not a quota, which
had been disallowed

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:09.850
in the precedent of
Bakke 25 years earlier.

00:31:09.850 --> 00:31:14.200
Now, because the case dealt
with higher education, colleges

00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:16.120
and grad schools,
like law schools--

00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:20.470
Justice O'Connor drew from
Powell's Bakke opinion

00:31:20.470 --> 00:31:23.920
to argue that not one, but
two constitutional provisions,

00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:27.700
as I mentioned earlier, needed
to be balanced or reconciled

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:31.840
to determine if the law school's
use of race was justified.

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:34.270
Now, we've got the Equal
Protection Clause, which

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:36.400
I'm sure you're tired
of me mentioning,

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:37.900
but they're all over it.

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:40.150
You have, on the one hand,
the Equal Protection Clause

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:41.740
of the 14th Amendment.

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:46.150
But then you also have the First
Amendment's Free Speech Clause.

00:31:46.150 --> 00:31:47.710
Now why?

00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:51.430
Courts have long treated
colleges and universities

00:31:51.430 --> 00:31:55.840
as a special niche in
constitutional adjudication.

00:31:55.840 --> 00:31:57.880
The nature of
education, they believe,

00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:00.040
requires academic freedom.

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:02.560
And so what do they say?

00:32:02.560 --> 00:32:05.320
They respected the university's
need, the law school

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:07.090
in this case, to
determine how best

00:32:07.090 --> 00:32:12.280
to promote the exchange of ideas
necessary for the education

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:13.750
of their students.

00:32:13.750 --> 00:32:17.080
If racial diversity could
help in this regard,

00:32:17.080 --> 00:32:19.120
the Court was not
going to second guess.

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:20.140
This is important.

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:24.630
They were going to
defer to Michigan's

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:27.540
educational autonomy,
as they called it,

00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:29.670
as it pursued what
they considered

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:33.540
to be a compelling state
interest, even though you've

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:36.270
got this constitutional
requirement that no state shall

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:40.660
deny to any person the equal
protection of the laws.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:44.400
So in satisfying the first prong
of the Strict Scrutiny Test,

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:47.640
let me make a few observations
about O'Connor's reasoning.

00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:49.800
First, she presumes--
and she calls it

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:53.730
this-- she presumes good faith
on the part of the university

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:55.170
when it claims
that hey, hey, hey,

00:32:55.170 --> 00:33:01.690
we're only using race to
promote this educational end.

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:03.190
Now, what's interesting
about this--

00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:06.340
if the Court is deferring
to them, if you will,

00:33:06.340 --> 00:33:10.630
granting them large
discretion in its use of race,

00:33:10.630 --> 00:33:14.380
doesn't that sound an awful lot
like Justice Brown's reasoning

00:33:14.380 --> 00:33:16.210
in Plessy?

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:19.240
Second, because the
Bakke precedent from 1978

00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:21.308
ruled that redressing
historical wrongs--

00:33:21.308 --> 00:33:23.350
the thing that you would
think affirmative action

00:33:23.350 --> 00:33:25.120
would be all about--

00:33:25.120 --> 00:33:26.920
Justice Powell said, no.

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:30.190
You can't claim that your
specific affirmative action

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:32.590
policy is going to
redress or provide

00:33:32.590 --> 00:33:34.840
a remedy for
historical harms that

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:36.980
were done to racial minorities.

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:41.290
So he actually said, you can't
justify racial classifications

00:33:41.290 --> 00:33:44.170
by saying we're trying
to repair the damage

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:46.730
of previous discrimination.

00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:52.670
The only purpose for Powell,
and O'Connor borrowed this--

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:55.280
the only purpose that
passed constitutional muster

00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:59.900
was racial diversity
as a means of promoting

00:33:59.900 --> 00:34:02.420
educational diversity.

00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:05.360
What's curious here
is Michigan provides

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:11.060
a counterintuitive explanation
for its promotion of diversity.

00:34:11.060 --> 00:34:12.620
Here it is.

00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:14.929
They argued that they
needed to include

00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:20.239
more underrepresented minorities
to break down stereotypes.

00:34:20.239 --> 00:34:23.060
You don't want to have a token
Native American or Black person

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:26.389
or Hispanic in class,
because otherwise,

00:34:26.389 --> 00:34:28.639
the majority white student
body would think, oh, yeah,

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:30.679
that's the way all Blacks think.

00:34:30.679 --> 00:34:33.840
In short, you see what they did?

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:36.300
Affirmative action
is justified not

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:38.760
because it helps
racial minorities

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:41.100
but because it helps
the white kids.

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:44.469
That's an interesting
turn of events.

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:47.870
What O'Connor called
effective participation

00:34:47.870 --> 00:34:50.350
she saw was promoted
by a law school that

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:52.239
enrolled a sufficient number--

00:34:52.239 --> 00:34:55.540
meaningful
representation-- of URMs.

00:34:55.540 --> 00:34:58.000
This showed, in her mind,
the path of leadership

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:02.530
being visibly open to talented
and qualified individuals

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:04.780
of every race and ethnicity.

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:06.940
And although O'Connor
claimed that race was not

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:10.420
to be understood as a
proxy for a specific way

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:13.120
that all members of
that race thought,

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:16.540
a critical mass of URMs
lets the law school

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:19.810
demonstrate visibly
that it is training

00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:23.440
a diverse group of
attorneys and therefore

00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:26.020
leading to the enhancement
of the integrity

00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:28.930
and trustworthiness of those
who will eventually assume roles

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:31.940
of leadership in the nation.

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:35.720
Now, satisfied the first
prong of strict scrutiny.

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:38.380
What about that
narrowly tailored part?

00:35:38.380 --> 00:35:41.960
We're moving from ends to means.

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:44.090
The 14th Amendment
protects, and O'Connor

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:45.920
is a stickler for this--

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:49.400
it protects individual
persons, not groups.

00:35:49.400 --> 00:35:52.160
Therefore, affirmative
action that is constitutional

00:35:52.160 --> 00:35:55.160
must ensure what
O'Connor, again drawing

00:35:55.160 --> 00:35:57.740
from the Bakke
opinion of Powell,

00:35:57.740 --> 00:36:02.160
called individualized
consideration.

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:04.800
Any policy that amounts to
a racial quota-- we're just

00:36:04.800 --> 00:36:08.520
going to set aside a certain
number of seats or a dual track

00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:13.230
admission system or any other
sort of mechanistic treatment

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:16.650
of applicants-- doesn't
treat each applicant

00:36:16.650 --> 00:36:20.010
as an individual person
and therefore, in her mind,

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:22.650
would violate the Equal
Protection Clause.

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:28.830
While race can act as a plus,
it cannot be what she called

00:36:28.830 --> 00:36:33.030
the defining feature of
a person's application.

00:36:33.030 --> 00:36:36.270
O'Connor's majority opinion
in Grutter v. Bollinger

00:36:36.270 --> 00:36:39.900
was a split decision, five
supporting affirmative action

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:43.570
at the law school and
four arguing to reject it.

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:45.930
That's a pretty tight,
pretty close decision.

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:50.720
So this made, though,
Grutter stronger than Bakke,

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:53.000
because that Bakke precedent
was peculiar in the sense

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:56.615
that Powell had an opinion
that no other justice joined.

00:36:56.615 --> 00:36:58.740
You're like, well, how
could that be the precedent?

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:02.810
Well, four justices agreed
with part of Powell's decision

00:37:02.810 --> 00:37:05.150
or reasoning, and
four other justices

00:37:05.150 --> 00:37:06.740
agreed with a different part.

00:37:06.740 --> 00:37:10.610
And that's how Powell's
opinion became the precedent

00:37:10.610 --> 00:37:15.370
even though it wasn't
technically a majority opinion.

00:37:15.370 --> 00:37:17.800
Then Chief Justice
William Rehnquist,

00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:20.360
he dissented in
the Grutter case,

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:23.830
arguing that the close parallels
between the percentage of URMs

00:37:23.830 --> 00:37:29.320
who applied and the percentage
who were accepted in any given

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:33.520
year, year over year, proved
that the law school was

00:37:33.520 --> 00:37:38.110
in fact employing a quota
to reach its critical mass

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:39.190
of racial minorities.

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:41.065
In other words, it was
looking to see-- well,

00:37:41.065 --> 00:37:44.290
how many out of the
whole pool applied?

00:37:44.290 --> 00:37:48.730
OK, we need to make sure
roughly that many get accepted.

00:37:48.730 --> 00:37:52.060
And so, he says, man, this is an
obvious failure of the narrowly

00:37:52.060 --> 00:37:53.110
tailored test.

00:37:53.110 --> 00:37:55.570
They're not being
treated as individuals.

00:37:55.570 --> 00:37:57.130
As for the
undergraduate university

00:37:57.130 --> 00:38:00.940
use of race in admissions,
the Gratz v. Bollinger case,

00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:03.970
the Supreme Court ruled
that by awarding 20 points

00:38:03.970 --> 00:38:07.180
automatically to
every URM applicant,

00:38:07.180 --> 00:38:10.930
the University did not treat
them as individual persons,

00:38:10.930 --> 00:38:12.880
and therefore they
were using race

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:17.110
in a way that ensured
their acceptance.

00:38:17.110 --> 00:38:20.560
Rehnquist wrote the
majority opinion in Gratz,

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:24.850
but he had to follow the
Grutter precedent in order

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:26.290
to reach his conclusion.

00:38:26.290 --> 00:38:29.020
And it was a conclusion
that O'Connor joined,

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:32.185
and so that was a
6 to 3 decision.

00:38:32.185 --> 00:38:34.060
So to wrap things up
here, Frederick Douglass

00:38:34.060 --> 00:38:37.420
devoted his adult life to
getting white Americans,

00:38:37.420 --> 00:38:40.210
as he put it, to
trust the operation

00:38:40.210 --> 00:38:42.190
of its own principles.

00:38:42.190 --> 00:38:46.060
Or as King put it, all
we ask is that you be

00:38:46.060 --> 00:38:49.200
true to what you said on paper.

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:51.960
That paper was the
Declaration of Independence

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:54.270
and the Constitution,
which King viewed

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:57.690
as a promissory note to
which every American was

00:38:57.690 --> 00:38:59.870
to fall heir.

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:01.910
A colorblind
Constitution would seem

00:39:01.910 --> 00:39:06.020
to be a logical implementation
of the colorblind ideals

00:39:06.020 --> 00:39:08.180
of the Declaration
of Independence.

00:39:08.180 --> 00:39:09.620
But the Supreme
Court essentially

00:39:09.620 --> 00:39:11.960
has left us with two
conclusions about government

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:14.520
use of racial classifications
under the Constitution.

00:39:14.520 --> 00:39:18.290
Number one, government
can't pay attention to race

00:39:18.290 --> 00:39:20.630
when it involves
assigning students

00:39:20.630 --> 00:39:25.890
to public schools at the
K through 12 grade levels.

00:39:25.890 --> 00:39:30.290
Number two, government
can pay attention to race

00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:32.570
if it involves colleges
and graduate schools,

00:39:32.570 --> 00:39:36.770
like law schools, and their
exercise of academic freedom

00:39:36.770 --> 00:39:39.920
in pursuit of the
educational diversity

00:39:39.920 --> 00:39:42.730
that flows from
racial diversity.

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:45.800
In the end, as long as the
Supreme Court continues

00:39:45.800 --> 00:39:49.880
to apply a strict scrutiny test
to determine if affirmative

00:39:49.880 --> 00:39:53.000
action or any other
race-based program--

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:56.420
whether in college admissions,
hiring and promotion,

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:58.760
or awarding
government contracts--

00:39:58.760 --> 00:40:04.420
a colorblind Constitution
is nowhere in sight.

